{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782561652.321594, "event": "reflective_dataset_built", "iteration": 1, "candidate_idx": 0, "components": ["system_prompt"], "dataset": {"system_prompt": [{"Inputs": "Correct the following HTML code, ensuring it meets W3C validation standards, and check the output in a browser using different viewport sizes. Additionally, add an external CSS file to change the font color and size based on window size.\n\n<html>\n  <heade>\n    <title>Test Page</title>\n  </heade>\n  <body>\n    <p>This is a test.</p>\n  </body>\n</html> Include keyword internet in your response. Include keyword freedom in the 10-th sentence, as the 26-th word of that sentence. Do not include keywords ['camp', 'catch', 'friend', 'heavy'] in the response.", "Generated Outputs": "Here is the corrected HTML code that meets W3C validation standards, along with an external CSS file linked to adjust font color and size based on the viewport size.\n\n**Corrected HTML (index.html):**\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n  <title>Test Page</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>This is a test.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n**External CSS file (styles.css):**\n```css\n/* Default font color and size */\nbody {\n  font-size: 16px;\n  color: black;\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n}\n\n/* Smaller screens */\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 14px;\n    color: darkblue;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Medium screens */\n@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1024px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 18px;\n    color: darkgreen;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Larger screens */\n@media (min-width: 1025px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 20px;\n    color: maroon;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. The `<heade>` tag in the original snippet is corrected to `<head>`.\n2. Added `<!DOCTYPE html>` declaration and `lang=\"en\"` attribute for better accessibility and proper HTML5 structure.\n3. Included `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` and viewport settings for responsive behavior.\n4. Linked an external CSS file (`styles.css`) that changes font color and size based on the browser window’s width.\n5. You can test the output by opening the `index.html` file in any modern browser and resizing the window to observe changes in font style and color.\n\nThe internet provides freedom to access knowledge and tools that help developers ensure their code is both valid and adaptive, enhancing the user experience across devices. When you check the output in different viewport sizes in a browser, you will notice the seamless change in font properties delivering the freedom users expect to read comfortably on a range of devices.", "Feedback": "Satisfied 2/3 constraints.\n✓ [keywords:word_once] satisfied — Include keyword internet in your response.\n✗ [keywords:keyword_specific_position] FAILED — Include keyword freedom in the 10-th sentence, as the 26-th word of that sentence.\n✓ [keywords:forbidden_words] satisfied — Do not include keywords ['camp', 'catch', 'friend', 'heavy'] in the response."}]}}
{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782561659.853624, "event": "proposal_end", "iteration": 1, "new_instructions": {"system_prompt": "You are a careful and precise assistant tasked with correcting and enhancing HTML code to comply with web standards, implementing responsive design, and following strict keyword placement instructions.\n\nGiven a user input containing:\n\n- An HTML code snippet that may contain errors.\n- Explicit instructions to correct the HTML code according to W3C validation standards.\n- Requirements to add external CSS for responsive font styling (e.g., color and size changes based on viewport/window size).\n- Instructions to check the output in a browser at different viewport sizes.\n- Specific keyword inclusion and exclusion constraints. These can require:\n  - Including a specific word (e.g., \"internet\") somewhere in the response.\n  - Inserting another keyword (e.g., \"freedom\") into an exact sentence number and word position within your entire response.\n  - Avoiding specific forbidden words anywhere in your output.\n\nYour task is to:\n\n1. Carefully read the user’s HTML snippet, identify and correct all HTML structure errors to pass the W3C validator:\n    - Ensure `<!DOCTYPE html>` is present.\n    - Use proper opening and closing tags (e.g., `<head>` not `<heade>`).\n    - Include essential accessibility and compatibility tags, such as `lang=\"en\"`, `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`, and viewport meta.\n    - Link the provided external CSS for responsive styling.\n2. Create an external CSS file (e.g., `styles.css`) that implements responsive font color and size changes using media queries for different window or device widths (such as mobile, tablet, and desktop breakpoints), as requested.\n3. Provide clear, well-formatted code blocks for both `index.html` (or similarly named file) and `styles.css`.\n4. Write a concise, informative explanation section summarizing:\n    - All corrections and enhancements made to the HTML.\n    - The usage and purpose of CSS media queries for responsiveness.\n    - Simple instructions for the user to test the output by resizing their browser window.\n5. Rigorously adhere to ALL keyword constraints:\n    - If requested to insert a keyword (e.g., \"freedom\") in the N-th sentence as the M-th word, ensure your final response strictly fulfills this placement, counting all sentences including explanations and instructions, and all words within those sentences.\n    - Include other specified keywords (e.g., \"internet\") at least once, anywhere in your response unless instructed to use a specific location.\n    - Never use any forbidden keywords provided in the input.\n6. Before submitting, double-check that all requested keywords are used exactly as instructed and that all forbidden words are omitted, regardless of phrasing or context.\n7. Ensure high-quality formatting, using Markdown code blocks for code and headings or bullet points for explanations if needed.\n\nBy following these steps, you will provide a helpful, standard-compliant, and precisely tailored HTML and CSS solution that meets advanced keyword placement instructions and all other user constraints."}, "prompts": {"system_prompt": "I provided an assistant with the following instructions to perform a task for me:\n```\nYou are a helpful assistant. Read the user's request carefully and follow every instruction and formatting constraint exactly.\n```\n\nThe following are examples of different task inputs provided to the assistant along with the assistant's response for each of them, and some feedback on how the assistant's response could be better:\n```\n# Example 1\n## Inputs\nCorrect the following HTML code, ensuring it meets W3C validation standards, and check the output in a browser using different viewport sizes. Additionally, add an external CSS file to change the font color and size based on window size.\n\n<html>\n  <heade>\n    <title>Test Page</title>\n  </heade>\n  <body>\n    <p>This is a test.</p>\n  </body>\n</html> Include keyword internet in your response. Include keyword freedom in the 10-th sentence, as the 26-th word of that sentence. Do not include keywords ['camp', 'catch', 'friend', 'heavy'] in the response.\n\n## Generated Outputs\nHere is the corrected HTML code that meets W3C validation standards, along with an external CSS file linked to adjust font color and size based on the viewport size.\n\n**Corrected HTML (index.html):**\n```html\n<!DOCTYPE html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"UTF-8\" />\n  <meta name=\"viewport\" content=\"width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0\" />\n  <title>Test Page</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\" />\n</head>\n<body>\n  <p>This is a test.</p>\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n**External CSS file (styles.css):**\n```css\n/* Default font color and size */\nbody {\n  font-size: 16px;\n  color: black;\n  font-family: Arial, sans-serif;\n}\n\n/* Smaller screens */\n@media (max-width: 600px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 14px;\n    color: darkblue;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Medium screens */\n@media (min-width: 601px) and (max-width: 1024px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 18px;\n    color: darkgreen;\n  }\n}\n\n/* Larger screens */\n@media (min-width: 1025px) {\n  body {\n    font-size: 20px;\n    color: maroon;\n  }\n}\n```\n\n### Explanation:\n1. The `<heade>` tag in the original snippet is corrected to `<head>`.\n2. Added `<!DOCTYPE html>` declaration and `lang=\"en\"` attribute for better accessibility and proper HTML5 structure.\n3. Included `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">` and viewport settings for responsive behavior.\n4. Linked an external CSS file (`styles.css`) that changes font color and size based on the browser window’s width.\n5. You can test the output by opening the `index.html` file in any modern browser and resizing the window to observe changes in font style and color.\n\nThe internet provides freedom to access knowledge and tools that help developers ensure their code is both valid and adaptive, enhancing the user experience across devices. When you check the output in different viewport sizes in a browser, you will notice the seamless change in font properties delivering the freedom users expect to read comfortably on a range of devices.\n\n## Feedback\nSatisfied 2/3 constraints.\n✓ [keywords:word_once] satisfied — Include keyword internet in your response.\n✗ [keywords:keyword_specific_position] FAILED — Include keyword freedom in the 10-th sentence, as the 26-th word of that sentence.\n✓ [keywords:forbidden_words] satisfied — Do not include keywords ['camp', 'catch', 'friend', 'heavy'] in the response.\n\n\n```\n\nYour task is to write a new instruction for the assistant.\n\nRead the inputs carefully and identify the input format and infer detailed task description about the task I wish to solve with the assistant.\n\nRead all the assistant responses and the corresponding feedback. Identify all niche and domain specific factual information about the task and include it in the instruction, as a lot of it may not be available to the assistant in the future. The assistant may have utilized a generalizable strategy to solve the task, if so, include that in the instruction as well.\n\nProvide the new instructions within ``` blocks."}, "raw_lm_outputs": {"system_prompt": "```\nYou are a careful and precise assistant tasked with correcting and enhancing HTML code to comply with web standards, implementing responsive design, and following strict keyword placement instructions.\n\nGiven a user input containing:\n\n- An HTML code snippet that may contain errors.\n- Explicit instructions to correct the HTML code according to W3C validation standards.\n- Requirements to add external CSS for responsive font styling (e.g., color and size changes based on viewport/window size).\n- Instructions to check the output in a browser at different viewport sizes.\n- Specific keyword inclusion and exclusion constraints. These can require:\n  - Including a specific word (e.g., \"internet\") somewhere in the response.\n  - Inserting another keyword (e.g., \"freedom\") into an exact sentence number and word position within your entire response.\n  - Avoiding specific forbidden words anywhere in your output.\n\nYour task is to:\n\n1. Carefully read the user’s HTML snippet, identify and correct all HTML structure errors to pass the W3C validator:\n    - Ensure `<!DOCTYPE html>` is present.\n    - Use proper opening and closing tags (e.g., `<head>` not `<heade>`).\n    - Include essential accessibility and compatibility tags, such as `lang=\"en\"`, `<meta charset=\"UTF-8\">`, and viewport meta.\n    - Link the provided external CSS for responsive styling.\n2. Create an external CSS file (e.g., `styles.css`) that implements responsive font color and size changes using media queries for different window or device widths (such as mobile, tablet, and desktop breakpoints), as requested.\n3. Provide clear, well-formatted code blocks for both `index.html` (or similarly named file) and `styles.css`.\n4. Write a concise, informative explanation section summarizing:\n    - All corrections and enhancements made to the HTML.\n    - The usage and purpose of CSS media queries for responsiveness.\n    - Simple instructions for the user to test the output by resizing their browser window.\n5. Rigorously adhere to ALL keyword constraints:\n    - If requested to insert a keyword (e.g., \"freedom\") in the N-th sentence as the M-th word, ensure your final response strictly fulfills this placement, counting all sentences including explanations and instructions, and all words within those sentences.\n    - Include other specified keywords (e.g., \"internet\") at least once, anywhere in your response unless instructed to use a specific location.\n    - Never use any forbidden keywords provided in the input.\n6. Before submitting, double-check that all requested keywords are used exactly as instructed and that all forbidden words are omitted, regardless of phrasing or