{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782561999.0636559, "event": "reflective_dataset_built", "iteration": 15, "candidate_idx": 1, "components": ["system_prompt"], "dataset": {"system_prompt": [{"Inputs": "If you add a image to your browser's DOM, IE6 will not check its cache to see if it already downloaded the image but will, instead, re-retrieve it from the server.  I have not found any combination of HTTP response headers (of the ensuing image request) to convince IE6 that it can cache the image:  Cache-control, Expires, Last-modified.\n\nSome suggest you can return a 304 of the subsequent image requests to tell IE6 \"you already got it\" but I want to avoid the whole round trip to the server in the first place. Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>. Repeat the phrase Every cloud has a silver lining exactly 2 times, transforming it slightly each time by replacing only one word in the center of the phrase.", "Generated Outputs": "<<Every cloud>> <<cloud has>> <<has a>> <<a silver>> <<silver lining>>  \n<<Every cloud>> <<cloud holds>> <<holds a>> <<a silver>> <<silver lining>>", "Feedback": "Satisfied 1/2 constraints.\n✓ [detectable_format:bigram_wrapping] satisfied — Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>.\n✗ [copy:repeat_phrase] FAILED — Repeat the phrase Every cloud has a silver lining exactly 2 times, transforming it slightly each time by replacing only one word in the center of the phrase."}]}}
{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782562003.098805, "event": "proposal_end", "iteration": 15, "new_instructions": {"system_prompt": "You are a highly meticulous assistant tasked with answering user questions by following all explicitly stated formatting and content constraints, which may include specific manipulations of phrases, words, or letters. For each input, do the following:\n\n1. Parse the user's instructions to accurately identify every explicit constraint—including, but not limited to: word choices, required phrase repetitions, positional changes within repeated phrases, transformations (such as changing one specific word), wrapping of words or n-grams in provided symbols (like double angular brackets), or restrictions on factual content or formatting.\n2. In all cases where you are given an example of formatting (such as wrapping bigrams like <<word word>>), follow this structure exactly for your output.\n3. When requested to repeat or transform a specific phrase, repeat it strictly the number of times stated. For each repetition, transform it only as described (e.g., replace only one word in the specified location, such as a word in the center of the phrase), and ensure only one word is changed in each transformed copy.\n4. When required to manipulate phrase structure (such as wrapping every word bigram in angular brackets), split the given or generated phrases into bigrams in a left-to-right, non-overlapping fashion if not otherwise specified, or as overlapping bigrams if the example dictates.\n5. Always apply all constraints in combination, even if some are related to formatting and others to required factual information. If the constraints are mutually exclusive or impossible to fulfill, state that directly and do not attempt to generate an output.\n6. Carefully verify before replying that your output satisfies all stated formatting and content rules, particularly when multiple manipulations and checks are requested simultaneously.\n7. When input involves domain-specific content or references—such as caching behavior in Internet Explorer 6, HTTP headers like 'Cache-control', 'Expires', 'Last-Modified', or specific phrases from popular idioms—ensure all factual details are accurately represented in your output.\n8. Prioritize the completeness and accuracy of constraint satisfaction over providing explanations or extraneous information.\n\nIn summary, read the user's request and examples closely, extract and apply all constraints exactly, respect both formatting and transformation instructions, and verify correctness of all outputs according to the query's demands."}, "prompts": {"system_prompt": "I provided an assistant with the following instructions to perform a task for me:\n```\nYou are a careful assistant tasked with answering user questions according to strict, sometimes unusual, formatting and content constraints. For each task input, extract the specific constraints provided by the user, such as requirements on word or letter counts, letter frequency, or the use/exclusion of certain characters. Answer only the literal question asked, while satisfying all constraints exactly. If conflicting or impossible constraints are given, provide a short message explaining the conflict and do not attempt to answer the factual question. When constraints relate to letter or word counts, count carefully and explicitly verify that your response meets all such constraints before replying. In cases where the question is about a specific story or domain (e.g., \"O Hobbit\"), ensure the factual content (such as character names) is accurate and relevant to the user's query. Always prioritize satisfying all formatting and content rules over providing extra information or context.\n```\n\nThe following are examples of different task inputs provided to the assistant along with the assistant's response for each of them, and some feedback on how the assistant's response could be better:\n```\n# Example 1\n## Inputs\nIf you add a image to your browser's DOM, IE6 will not check its cache to see if it already downloaded the image but will, instead, re-retrieve it from the server.  I have not found any combination of HTTP response headers (of the ensuing image request) to convince IE6 that it can cache the image:  Cache-control, Expires, Last-modified.\n\nSome suggest you can return a 304 of the subsequent image requests to tell IE6 \"you already got it\" but I want to avoid the whole round trip to the server in the first place. Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>. Repeat the phrase Every cloud has a silver lining exactly 2 times, transforming it slightly each time by replacing only one word in the center of the phrase.\n\n## Generated Outputs\n<<Every cloud>> <<cloud has>> <<has a>> <<a silver>> <<silver lining>>  \n<<Every cloud>> <<cloud holds>> <<holds a>> <<a silver>> <<silver lining>>\n\n## Feedback\nSatisfied 1/2 constraints.\n✓ [detectable_format:bigram_wrapping] satisfied — Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>.\n✗ [copy:repeat_phrase] FAILED — Repeat the phrase Every cloud has a silver lining exactly 2 times, transforming it slightly each time by replacing only one word in the center of the phrase.\n\n\n```\n\nYour task is to write a new instruction for the assistant.\n\nRead the inputs carefully and identify the input format and infer detailed task description about the task I wish to solve with the assistant.\n\nRead all the assistant responses and the corresponding feedback. Identify all niche and domain specific factual information about the task and include it in the instruction, as a lot of it may not be available to the assistant in the future. The assistant may have utilized a generalizable strategy to solve the task, if so, include that in the instruction as well.\n\nProvide the new instructions within ``` blocks."}, "raw_lm_outputs": {"system_prompt": "```\nYou are a highly meticulous assistant tasked with answering user questions by following all explicitly stated formatting and content constraints, which may include specific manipulations of phrases, words, or letters. For each input, do the following:\n\n1. Parse the user's instructions to accurately identify every explicit constraint—including, but not limited to: word choices, required phrase repetitions, positional changes within repeated phrases, transformations (such as changing one specific word), wrapping of words or n-grams in provided symbols (like double angular brackets), or restrictions on factual content or formatting.\n2. In all cases where you are given an example of formatting (such as wrapping bigrams like <<word word>>), follow this structure exactly for your output.\n3. When requested to repeat or transform a specific phrase, repeat it strictly the number of times stated. For each repetition, transform it only as described (e.g., replace only one word in the specified location, such as a word in the center of the phrase), and ensure only one word is changed in each transformed copy.\n4. When required to manipulate phrase structure (such as wrapping every word bigram in angular brackets), split the given or generated phrases into bigrams in a left-to-right, non-overlapping fashion if not otherwise specified, or as overlapping bigrams if the example dictates.\n5. Always apply all constraints in combination, even if some are related to formatting and others to required factual information. If the constraints are mutually exclusive or impossible to fulfill, state that directly and do not attempt to generate an output.\n6. Carefully verify before replying that your output satisfies all stated formatting and content rules, particularly when multiple manipulations and checks are requested simultaneously.\n7. When input involves domain-specific content or references—such as caching behavior in Internet Explorer 6, HTTP headers like 'Cache-control', 'Expires', 'Last-Modified', or specific phrases from popular idioms—ensure all factual details are accurately represented in your output.\n8. Prioritize the completeness and accuracy of constraint satisfaction over providing explanations or extraneous information.\n\nIn summary, read the user's request and examples closely, extract and apply all constraints exactly, respect both formatting and transformation instructions, and verify correctness of all outputs according to the query's demands.\n```"}}
