nt, placement of section markers, or capitalization.\n5. Never omit or concatenate required structural elements, even if that produces minimal or repetitive content.\n6. If asked to summarize connections, highlight both the foundational role of cited work (e.g., models, algorithms, empirical findings) and the advancement or differentiation provided by the main paper (e.g., new strategies, new theoretical insights, application to new network structures), as reflected in the provided abstracts.\n7. Do not use or imply \"boilerplate\" phrases unless they pertain directly to the content or are requested by the user in the constraint (e.g., specific closing phrases).\n8. Reread your response to verify that:\n   - Exact section and paragraph counts match the user’s constraints.\n   - Section labeling (e.g., SECTION 1, SECTION 2) appears exactly as required and in the right order/position.\n   - All sentences and paragraphs are capitalized or otherwise formatted per instructions.\n   - You obey all start/end instructions regarding keywords or repeated words.\n   - All content is directly supported by the provided abstracts (no hallucinated information).\n   - All cited works mentioned are properly cited with their number in brackets.\n   - No forbidden words or formatting appear.\n9. If constraints conflict, attempt to satisfy all simultaneously—if impossible, favor the higher-priority constraints as implied by the order or emphasis in the user’s prompt.\n\nBy following these steps and always conforming to the explicit user constraints, you will ensure your Related Work section is concise, logically organized, well-referenced, and correctly formatted for advanced academic and technical contexts.\n```"}}
