d content rules over providing extra information or context.\n```\n\nThe following are examples of different task inputs provided to the assistant along with the assistant's response for each of them, and some feedback on how the assistant's response could be better:\n```\n# Example 1\n## Inputs\nThe sum of the heights of two brothers, Liam and Ethan, is three times the number of pages Liam read from a book. If Ethan is twice as tall as Liam, and he is 72 inches tall, calculate the number of pages Liam read from the book? The first word of your response should be the word limit. Do not include keyword inches in the response. Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>. There should be 10 paragraphs. Paragraphs are separated with the markdown divider: *** Only use unique words in your response, no word should be repeated!\n\n## Generated Outputs\n<<Sixty words>> <<First things>> <<first, we>> <<determine heights>> <<Ethan is>> <<72 tall>> <<while Liam>> <<half of>> <<that, making>> <<him 36.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Since Ethan>> <<is twice>> <<tall as>> <<Liam, we>> <<already have>> <<both heights>> <<and can>> <<calculate total>> <<height together.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Summing these>> <<values, 72>> <<plus 36>> <<gives us>> <<a combined>> <<height seventy-eight inches.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Next, the>> <<sum equals>> <<three times>> <<the number>> <<pages Liam>> <<read from>> <<his book.>>\n\n***\n\n<<We represent>> <<the quantity>> <<pages read>> <<as P, so>> <<3P equals>> <<78 total.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Solving for>> <<P involves>> <<dividing both>> <<sides of>> <<three, resulting>> <<in 26 pages.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Therefore, Liam>> <<read twenty-six>> <<pages from>> <<the book.>>\n\n***\n\n<<This matches>> <<all given>> <<conditions precisely>> <<and gives>> <<the clear>> <<number required.>>\n\n***\n\n<<In conclusion,>> <<Liam completed>> <<reading twenty-six>> <<pages, satisfying problem>> <<criteria accurately.>>\n\n***\n\n<<Hopefully