rror:\n        return \"Error: Invalid input.\"\n    \n    # Check if the value is within the range\n    if not low <= value <= high:\n        return \"Error: Out of range.\"\n    \n    # Rounding process\n    decimal = value - int(value)\n    if decimal >= 0.5 and value >= 0:\n        value += 1\n    elif decimal <= -0.5 and value <= 0:\n        value -= 1\n    \n    return int(value)\nI'd love some insight into how the translated code works. Include keyword glass in the 4-th sentence, as the 8-th word of that sentence.\n\n## Generated Outputs\n```rust\nfn special_rounding(value: &str, low: f64, high: f64) -> Result<i32, &'static str> {\n    // Input Validation\n    let mut num: f64 = match value.parse() {\n        Ok(v) => v,\n        Err(_) => return Err(\"Error: Invalid input.\"),\n    };\n\n    // Check if the value is within the range\n    if num < low || num > high {\n        return Err(\"Error: Out of range.\");\n    }\n\n    // Rounding process\n    let decimal = num - num.trunc();\n    if decimal >= 0.5 && num >= 0.0 {\n        num += 1.0;\n    } else if decimal <= -0.5 && num <= 0.0 {\n        num -= 1.0;\n    }\n\n    Ok(num as i32)\n}\n```\n\nThis Rust function performs a similar operation as the given Python `special_rounding` function. First, it attempts to parse the input string to a floating-point number, returning an error if it fails. Then, it verifies that the number lies within the specified `low` and `high` bounds, returning an error if out of range. This process acts like looking through a glass carefully examining the input to ensure correctness. Lastly, it calculates the decimal portion and applies conditional adjustments to the integer part before returning the rounded integer in a Result type. That's my answer.\n\n## Feedback\nSatisfied 0/1 constraints.\n✗ [keywords:keyword_specific_position] FAILED — Include keyword glass in the 4-th sentence, as the 8-th word of that sentence.\n\n\n```\n\nYour task is to write a new instruction for the a