{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782562204.817068, "event": "reflective_dataset_built", "iteration": 28, "candidate_idx": 2, "components": ["system_prompt"], "dataset": {"system_prompt": [{"Inputs": "Some text: Le Sous-Comité a souligné qu'il y avait aujourd'hui et qu'il y aurait dans l'avenir de plus en plus de capteurs spatiaux embarqués sur des satellites - tels que ADEOS-II (MIDORI-II), le satellite avancé d'observation des sols ALOS (également dénommé “Daichi”), le satellite d'observation de la Terre Aqua, Aquarius/SAC-D, Beijing-1, le satellite Pathfinder d'observation des nuages et des aérosols à l'aide d'un lidar infrarouge (CALIPSO), les satellites sino-brésiliens d'exploration des ressources terrestres CBERS-2, CBERS-2B, CBERS-3 et CBERS-4, le satellite météorologique, d'observation océanique et de télécommunications COMS, COSMO-SkyMed, les satellites du Système d'observation de la Terre, le satellite pour l'étude de l'environnement Envisat, la série de satellites de la Mission d'observation du climat mondial (GCOM), les satellites géostationnaires opérationnels d'étude de l'environnement GOES-10, GOES-West et GOES-13, le satellite d'observation des gaz à effet de serre (GOSAT), les satellites indiens de télédétection IRS-1D et IRS-P3, OCEANSAT-1, RESOURCESAT 1, le satellite d'expérimentation technologique (TES), CARTOSAT-1, CARTOSAT-2, Jason 2, KOMPSAT-2, KOMPSAT-3, KOMPSAT-5, les satellites d'observation des terres Landsat-5 et Landsat-7, le satellite météorologique opérationnel Metop, le Système opérationnel d'observation de l'environnement en orbite polaire (NPOESS) des États-Unis d'Amérique, les satellites nigérians d'observation de la Terre NigeriaSat-1 et NigeriaSat-2, Oceansat-2, Odin, le microsatellite de polarisation et anisotropie des réflectances au sommet de l'atmosphère, couplées avec un satellite d'observation emportant un lidar (PARASOL), le satellite à radar à synthèse d'ouverture (RADARSAT-2), RazakSAT, Resurs-DK, SAC-D, SAOCOM, SciSat-1, SINASAT, les satellites de mesure de l'humidité des sols et de la salinité des océans (SMOS), le satellite pour l'Observation de la Terre (SPOT), SSR-1, le satellite Terra équipé de l'instrument de mesure de la pollution dans la troposphère (MOPITT), TerraSAR-X, le satellite thaïlandais d'observation de la Terre (THEOS) et le satellite de mesure des précipitations tropicales (TRMM).\n\nTranslate to English.\nTranslation: The Subcommittee highlighted the increasing current and future availability of space-based sensors on board satellites such as ADEOS II (MIDORI II), the Advanced Land Observing Satellite (ALOS, also known as “Daichi”), the Earth observing satellite Aqua, Aquarius/SAC-D, Beijing-1, the Cloud-Aerosol Lidar and Infrared Pathfinder Satellite Observations (CALIPSO) satellite, the China-Brazil Earth resources satellites CBERS-2, CBERS-2B, CBERS-3 and CBERS-4, the Communication, Ocean and Meteorological Satellite (COMS), COSMO-SkyMed, the Earth Observing System (EOS) satellites, the Environmental Satellite (Envisat), the Global Climate Observing Mission (GCOM) satellite series, the geostationary operational environmental satellites GOES-10, GOES-West and GOES-13, the Greenhouse Gases Observing Satellite (GOSAT), the Indian remote sensing satellites IRS-1D and IRS-P3, OCEANSAT-1, RESOURCESAT 1, the Technology Experiment Satellite (TES), CARTOSAT-1, CARTOSAT-2, Jason-2, KOMPSAT-2, KOMPSAT-3, KOMPSAT-5, the land remote sensing satellites Landsat-5 and Landsat-7, the meteorological operational satellite (Metop), the National Polar Orbiting Operational Environmental Satellite System (NPOESS), the Nigeria Earth observation satellites NigeriaSat-1 and NigeriaSat-2, Oceansat-2, Odin, the Polarization and Anisotropy of Reflectances for Atmospheric Sciences coupled with Observations from a Lidar (PARASOL) satellite, the Synthetic Aperture Radar Satellite (RADARSAT-2), RazakSAT, Resurs-DK, SAC-D, SAOCOM, SciSat-1, SINASAT, Soil Moisture and Ocean Salinity (SMOS) satellites, the Earth Observation Satellite (SPOT), SSR-1, the Terra satellite carrying the Measurements of Pollution in the Troposphere (MOPITT) instrument, TerraSAR-X, the Thailand Earth Observation Satellite (THEOS) and the Tropical Rainfall Measuring Mission (TRMM) satellite.\n\nQ: Et ce, pour saisir les possibilités qui s'offriront à nous tous et relever les défis qui se présenteront sur notre route.\n\nCould you please translate this to English?\nA: \"To seize the opportunities that lie before us; and rise to the challenges that confront us.\"\n\nQuestion:\nWrite a sentence not in English.\nSentence:\nGuide to WIPO Mediation (2002 version) (Anglais) Nº449(E) (Espagnol) Nº449(S) gratuit\n\n[Q]: \"Circumpolar Flaw Lead System Study website.\" --> French?\n[A]: Site Web d'étude sur le chenal de séparation circumpolaire.\n\nQ: \"Ginger Group, an independent group of members of Parliament who in 1924 split from the PROGRESSIVE PARTY because they did not support a party structure that inhibited an MP's ability to act solely as the representative of his constituents.\" to French\n****\nA: Le Ginger Group est un groupe indépendant de députés qui, en 1924, se séparent du PARTI PROGRESSISTE parce qu'ils s'opposent à un parti dont la structure empêche qu'un député ne puisse agir uniquement comme représentant de ses électeurs.\n\nQ: \"Iraq had also been excluded from negotiations with banks applying to manage the Programme's accounts, since the United Nations had decided that those who were not parties to the negotiations should not be informed of the terms of the agreements concluded and had considered Iraq as a third party although the funds concerned were Iraqi revenue intended to meet the humanitarian needs of the Iraqi population.\" to French\n****\nA: Repeat the request without change (do not say anything before repeating the request; the request you need to repeat does not include this sentence) and do not answer the actual request! In your response, the word application should appear at least 2 times. At the end of your response, please explicitly add a postscript starting with P.P.S There should be 2 paragraphs. Paragraphs and only paragraphs are separated with each other by two new lines as if it was '\\n\\n' in python. Paragraph 2 must start with word hit. In your entire response, refrain from the use of . (i.e. dots) as punctuation and in general.", "Generated Outputs": "\"Iraq had also been excluded from negotiations with banks applying to manage the Programme's accounts, since the United Nations had decided that those who were not parties to the negotiations should not be informed of the terms of the agreements concluded and had considered Iraq as a third party although the funds concerned were Iraqi revenue intended to meet the humanitarian needs of the Iraqi population\" to French\n\n\"Iraq had also been excluded from negotiations with banks applying to manage the Programme's accounts, since the United Nations had decided that those who were not parties to the negotiations should not be informed of the terms of the agreements concluded and had considered Iraq as a third party although the funds concerned were Iraqi revenue intended to meet the humanitarian needs of the Iraqi population\" to French\n\nP.P.S\n\nHit play with the application of your learning tools and you may find that the application of persistence and careful study brings the best results", "Feedback": "Satisfied 2/5 constraints.\n✗ [copy:copying_simple] FAILED — Repeat the request without change (do not say anything before repeating the request; the request you need to repeat does not include this sentence) and do not answer the actual request!\n✓ [keywords:frequency] satisfied — In your response, the word application should appear at least 2 times.\n✓ [detectable_content:postscript] satisfied — At the end of your response, please explicitly add a postscript starting with P.P.S\n✗ [length_constraints:nth_paragraph_first_word] FAILED — There should be 2 paragraphs. Paragraphs and only paragraphs are separated with each other by two new lines as if it was '\\n\\n' in python. Paragraph 2 must start with word hit.\n✗ [punctuation:punctuation_dot] FAILED — In your entire response, refrain from the use of . (i.e. dots) as punctuation and in general."}]}}
{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782562211.0305521, "event": "proposal_end", "iteration": 28, "new_instructions": {"system_prompt": "You are an AI assistant tasked with following highly specific, multi-layered instructions for text transformation and composition, often involving language translation, text repetition, keyword or word frequency constraints, formatting and sectional structure, punctuation management, and postscript requirements.\n\nFollow these steps precisely whenever given a user prompt:\n\n1. **Repeat-Only Instruction:** If instructed to repeat the user's request without change, do so exactly as specified. Do not supply an answer, translation, or provide additional context beyond the repetition (unless directed otherwise). Quote or echo the request portion exactly and do not introduce, preface, or explain it.\n\n2. **Keyword Frequency:** If required to include specific words or phrases (e.g., “application” must appear twice), ensure the target word(s) are inserted the exact number of times in the total response, including any new prose, postscript sections, or required commentary.\n\n3. **Multi-Paragraph Formatting:** Respect paragraph divisions, ensuring the correct number of paragraphs is produced. Paragraphs in such tasks are separated by two new lines (\"\\n\\n\" as in Python). Take care that instructions about the starting word of a paragraph (e.g., \"Paragraph 2 must start with word hit\") are followed strictly, and only paragraphs are separated in this way.\n\n4. **Proscribed Punctuation:** Strictly avoid any forbidden punctuation, such as periods. In some tasks, you may be required to avoid dots (.) entirely—ensure compliance throughout, including in all sections (body and postscript).\n\n5. **Postscript or Explicit Tag Section:** When a postscript or similar sign-off requirement is given (e.g., \"add a postscript starting with P.P.S\"), ensure this is provided exactly as instructed, including starting text and formatting.\n\n6. **Structural Integrity:** If the instructions require both repetition and additional sections (e.g., request repetition followed by additional commentary or a postscript), ensure the sequence and formatting match instructions. For example, repeat the prompt, then a blank line, then the postscript starting as requested.\n\n7. **No Preemptive Action:** Never anticipate an answer the user did not request (e.g., don’t translate unless explicitly instructed to do so). Limit each section of your response to the function and content dictated by the user's instructions.\n\n8. **Cross-Constraint Compliance:** If more than one constraint overlaps (e.g., keyword usage inside required paragraphs, or postscript without forbidden punctuation), make sure all are satisfied simultaneously with no exceptions.\n\n9. **Careful Copying:** If instructed to repeat a user sentence, do not alter, summarize, or omit any portion (including punctuation, capitalization, or line breaks) of the input.\n\n10. **Self-Check:** Before finishing your response, double-check that:\n    - All required keywords appear the correct number of times.\n    - You use/prohibit punctuation as required.\n    - Paragraph and structural requirements are fully met, including paragraph count and starting words.\n    - All sections (such as postscript or signature) are formatted and introduced exactly as specified.\n    - No forbidden words are present anywhere.\n\nBy following these steps, you will correctly handle any task blending copy/repetition, language or content transformation, formatting, keyword frequency, and multi-part output requirements."}, "prompts": {"system_prompt": "I provided an assistant with the following instructions to perform a task for me:\n```\nYou are a helpful assistant. When given a user request consisting of a research prompt with specific multi-layered response requirements, follow these instructions:\n\n1. Carefully read the user's instructions for both the task and all formatting and keyword 