erwise) text input—including punctuation, newlines, and non-word content—and process it so that every consecutive bigram is wrapped in << >>. \n\nHere are the steps you must follow:\n1. Read and preserve the original text, maintaining the line and paragraph breaks as in the input.\n2. Identify all *consecutive* word bigrams (sequences of two adjacent words) throughout the entire input, line by line and paragraph by paragraph. Words are defined as sequences of Sinhala (or other language) characters separated by whitespace and/or punctuation.\n3. For each consecutive bigram, wrap the two-word sequence in double angle brackets with a single space between the words, such as: <<word1 word2>>.\n4. The wrapping must only be applied to *word* bigrams. Ignore punctuation marks and do not include them inside the bigram brackets, except when they are directly attached to a word (such as contractions or clitics), or if punctuation is a word-internal character.\n5. Move through the text word by word in their natural order (left to right, top to bottom), wrapping every consecutive pair (overlapping). For a sequence of N words, you should output (N-1) bigrams, each in the required wrapping, and separated by the original spacing/newlines.\n6. Bigrams may overlap. For example, in \"මම නම් ගෙදර\", output <<මම නම්>> <<නම් ගෙදර>>.\n7. If the input text is only a single word or empty, you should output nothing.\n8. Output only the wrapped bigrams, separated by the original input text's spacing and newlines if relevant. Do not generate any additional commentary, introduction, or explanation before or after the wrapped bigrams.\n9. If the user specifies an exact phrase they require you to end with (e.g., \"That's all I have to say.\"), your output *must* conclude with that phrase, and you must not add any other words or commentary after that phrase.\n\nExamples:\nInput: \nමම නිවෙසේ ඉන්නෙමි.\nOutput: \n<<මම නිවෙසේ>> <<නิวෙසේ ඉන්නෙමි>>.\n\nInput:\nඔබ කුමක් කරමින් සිටීද? \nOutput:\n<<ඔබ කුමක