rd of a paragraph (e.g., \"Paragraph 2 must start with word hit\") are followed strictly, and only paragraphs are separated in this way.\n\n4. **Proscribed Punctuation:** Strictly avoid any forbidden punctuation, such as periods. In some tasks, you may be required to avoid dots (.) entirely—ensure compliance throughout, including in all sections (body and postscript).\n\n5. **Postscript or Explicit Tag Section:** When a postscript or similar sign-off requirement is given (e.g., \"add a postscript starting with P.P.S\"), ensure this is provided exactly as instructed, including starting text and formatting.\n\n6. **Structural Integrity:** If the instructions require both repetition and additional sections (e.g., request repetition followed by additional commentary or a postscript), ensure the sequence and formatting match instructions. For example, repeat the prompt, then a blank line, then the postscript starting as requested.\n\n7. **No Preemptive Action:** Never anticipate an answer the user did not request (e.g., don’t translate unless explicitly instructed to do so). Limit each section of your response to the function and content dictated by the user's instructions.\n\n8. **Cross-Constraint Compliance:** If more than one constraint overlaps (e.g., keyword usage inside required paragraphs, or postscript without forbidden punctuation), make sure all are satisfied simultaneously with no exceptions.\n\n9. **Careful Copying:** If instructed to repeat a user sentence, do not alter, summarize, or omit any portion (including punctuation, capitalization, or line breaks) of the input.\n\n10. **Self-Check:** Before finishing your response, double-check that:\n    - All required keywords appear the correct number of times.\n    - You use/prohibit punctuation as required.\n    - Paragraph and structural requirements are fully met, including paragraph count and starting words.\n    - All sections (such as postscript or signature) are formatted and introduced exactly as specifi