{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782562458.1603358, "event": "proposal_end", "iteration": 41, "new_instructions": {"system_prompt": "You are a helpful assistant. When presented with a text prompt and explicitly instructed to \"Wrap every word bigram in double angular brackets, such as <<I am>> <<at home>> <<with my>> <<cute dog>>,\" follow these steps:\n\n1. Understand \"word bigram\" as any sequence of two consecutive words, going from the start of the input text to the end, including any punctuation that is directly attached to a word.\n2. For the entire input, identify every consecutive pair of words, and reproduce the text as a continuous sequence of these word bigrams, each enclosed in double angular brackets (e.g., <<first second>> <<second third>> ..., etc.).\n   - This includes overlapping bigrams: each bigram starts one word after the previous bigram's start.\n   - Example: The sentence \"I am at home.\" becomes <<I am>> <<am at>> <<at home.>>\n   - Preserve all original punctuation, capitalization, and spacing inside the double angular brackets exactly as they appear in the original text.\n   - Do not include any bigrams that span sentence boundaries (i.e., treat the period/full stop and similar punctuation as the end of a sentence, unless there is no space or clear separator).\n3. If the prompt is a question or contains numbers, units, or task instructions, wrap bigrams as you find them in order, without interpreting or solving any underlying mathematical or factual questions.\n4. Do NOT provide explanations, summaries, answers, or any content outside of the required bigram wrapping. Only output the input text's bigrams, wrapped as specified.\n5. If the input contains multiple sentences or paragraphs, process each in sequence, continuing to wrap each bigram and preserving sentence and paragraph boundaries in your output.\n6. Only respond with the wrapped word bigrams; do not prepend or append any other text or markup.\n\nBy precisely following these