 with specific multi-layered response requirements, follow these instructions:\n\n1. Carefully read the user's instructions for both the task and all formatting and keyword constraints. These constraints may affect every part of your answer (structure, length, keywords, phrasing, formatting, and specific placeholders).\n2. When tasked to research historical figures from Idaho (or any domain-specific research), include at least three relevant individuals. For Idaho historical figures, commonly accepted examples are Sacagawea (b. 1788, d. 1812), William H. Sewell (b. 1845, d. 1901), and Martha Hughes Cannon (b. 1857, d. 1932).\n3. If the user requires the response to contain placeholders in square brackets (e.g., [address], [data]), ensure to include at least as many as specified. Placeholders should be represented by words or identifiers enclosed in square brackets, not actual data.\n4. If valid JSON is required, present the research results as a well-formed JSON object. Where placeholders are specified, include them as fields or values (e.g., \"unknown_field1\": \"[data]\").\n5. For keyword usage, insert each specified keyword the exact number of times required (for example, use \"smell\" once and \"human\" twice) and avoid any listed forbidden words.\n6. Adhere strictly to sentence count (e.g., \"exactly two sentences\") and any specific phrasing for the last word or concluding phrase (e.g., last word must be \"many\", or finish with \"That's my answer.\" with no further words).\n7. If asked, exclude the use of specific words (such as \"list\" or \"famous\") anywhere in your response.\n8. Double-check that you have satisfied every user constraint, including: correct number of paragraphs, sentences, or words; exact spelling and placement of required phrases; the required number of placeholders; word and keyword usage; forbidden words.\n9. If multiple formatting or content constraints overlap, ensure all are simultaneously satisfied without exception.\n\nBy following