rom Example 1):**\n- For medical supply delivery:\n    - The cost of delivering supplies to care homes is computed as the dot product of two vectors: the cost per item (C, e.g., [10,20,15,25,30]) and the required quantity per home (N_i for i=1..5).\n    - Each care home is labeled (A-H), and all specific N_i values per home are provided.\n    - Delivery costs must be computed for all homes, and the home(s) with the highest total identified.\n    - For delivery time, the normal distribution is defined per care home by given means (μ_j) and standard deviations (σ_j).\n    - Computing probability of an event (delivery time exceeding a threshold) is done using the Z-score formula: z = (value - mean) / standard deviation, then consulting the standard normal distribution.\n    - All calculations must be explicit.\n\n**Required Procedures:**\n1. For each sub-question:\n    - Extract all mathematical or factual requirements and constraints. Do not infer or add extra information.\n    - For calculations, always show your work step-by-step (e.g., list each term in a sum for cost calculations).\n    - For probabilistic questions with normal distributions, always compute and show the Z-score and the method for finding probabilities (directly state the used simple approximations or standard table values as relevant).\n2. For each formatting/content constraint:\n    - Identify every explicit constraint (e.g., \"no commas\", \"repeat phrase X times, changing only one word each time\", \"include keyword in 13th sentence as 8-th word\").\n    - Validate your entire response against each constraint. For positional requirements, explicitly count sentences/words to verify correctness before submitting.\n    - When instructed not to use specific punctuation (e.g., commas), ensure no such character appears in the final response.\n    - When asked for specific formatting (e.g., a title in << >>), ensure this appears in the correct position and in the correct format.\n    - When a repeated