{"run_id": "baseline_seed0_b1", "ts": 1782563217.038441, "event": "proposal_end", "iteration": 67, "new_instructions": {"system_prompt": "You are solving a probability and statistics word-problem involving the Poisson distribution and conditional probability, specifically in a sports context, and following a highly specific output format.\n\nTask Description and Domain Knowledge:\n- The scenario involves an ex-footballer who played in the 1966-67 Regional Championship, which lasted for a continuous period of 30 days starting January 1, 1967.\n- The footballer scored a random number of goals during these 30 days, following a Poisson distribution.\n- The *mean rate* at which he scored is given as 1.5 goals *per week*. Note: For these problems, 1 week is always treated as exactly 7 days.\n- For a Poisson distribution over a period, λ (the Poisson parameter) is computed as (rate per week) × (number of weeks in the time period).\n  - For example, over 30 days: number of weeks = 30/7 = 4.2857, so λ = 1.5 × (30/7).\n- The probability of a Poisson random variable X being exactly k is given by P(X=k) = exp(-λ) × λ^k / k! \n- When a Poisson event count is partitioned into disjoint intervals (e.g., first 10 days versus remaining 20 days), the conditional distribution of the number of events in a subinterval, given a fixed total, is binomial:\n  - If Y goals are scored in total over n days, and we want the probability that exactly k of those goals were scored in m of those n days, this is Binomial(Y, m/n).\n- The two required computations are:\n  1. The probability that the ex-footballer scored exactly 6 goals during the 30-day championship, using the Poisson formula for k = 6 and λ = 1.5 × (30/7).\n  2. Given exactly 6 goals were scored in total, find the conditional probability that at least 2 of them were scored in the first 10 days. This is a binomial problem with parameters n = 6, p = 10/30 = 1/3, and k ≥ 2.\n\nOutput Format:\n- Your response must be in exactly one section, but that section may have multiple paragraphs for clarity.\n- Each paragraph must begin with the phrase \"ParagraphX\" (e.g., Paragraph1, Paragraph2), with NO space between \"Paragraph\" and the number.\n- Within each paragraph, all sentences must be connected by hyphens, with no spaces on either side of the hyphens.\n- Every sentence must end with the word \"panic\", appearing just before any closing punctuation mark (if present).\n- Do not use any periods/full stops (“.”) anywhere in the output, not even in decimal numbers; use \"point\" instead (for example, write \"1 point 5\" instead of \"1.5\").\n- All calculations, reasoning steps, and explanations must be included, and math notation and equations should be provided inline in standard text.\n- The output must be fully self-contained, walk through the required reasoning, and strictly obey all formatting, punctuation, and vocabulary requirements as outlined above."}, "prompts": {"system_prompt": "I provided an assistant with the following instructions to perform a task for me:\n```\nYou are solving a probability and statistics task focused on the Poisson distribution and conditional probability.\n\nTask Description:\n- The scenario involves an ex-footballer who played in the 1966-67 Regional Championship. He is now 60 years old. \n- The period in question is a continuous 30-day championship, starting on January 1, 1967.\n- The number of goals the ex-footballer scored during these 30 days follows a Poisson distribution with a mean rate (λ) of 1.5 goals per week.\n- There are two main questions to answer:\n  1. Calculate the probability the ex-footballer scored exactly 6 goals during the championship.\n  2. Given that exactly 6 goals were scored in total, find the conditional probability that at least 2 of those goals were scored in the first 10 days of the championship.\n- You may use properties of the Poisson distribution, as well as relevant properties of the conditional and binomial distributions for calculations, as appropriate.\n\nOutput Format Instructions:\n- Your response must be organized into exactly one section, but this section may contain multiple paragraphs as needed for clarity.\n- Each section (paragraph) must begin with the phrase \"Paragraph X\" where X is its order in the sequence, with no space between “Paragraph” and the number (e.g., Paragraph1, Paragraph2, etc.).\n- Within each paragraph, all sentences must be connected using hyphens, with no spaces between them.\n- The last word of every sentence, just before any closing punctuation (if any), must be the word \"panic\".\n- Use explanation in natural language and show the necessary equations and probability notation for each computation, formatted inline using standard text.\n- Do not use any periods/full stops (“.”) anywhere in your output, not even in abbreviations or as decimal points (use \"point\" instead).\n- Do not break from the constraint of all sentences ending with \"panic\" (i.e., make sure \"panic\" is the last word before a hyphen or closing punctuation).\n- Do not include any extra explanation or meta-text except for what is required to answer the question.\n- Your output should be self-contained, step by step, and provide the required calculations, keeping all formatting and ending requirements strictly.\n\nDomain Knowledge Guidance:\n- Remember, a Poisson random variable with rate λ for time t computes as λ = rate per unit time × duration (in units).\n- For Poisson distribution, probability of k events is P(X=k) = exp(-λ) × λ^k / k!\n- When a total Poisson count is given across intervals and you want conditional event counts in a subinterval, use the Binomial distribution with the probability equal to the fraction of λ in the subinterval over total λ.\n- Clearly show parameters chosen and steps in reasoning.\n\nBy following these instructions precisely, your output will fulfill all paragraphing, formatting, domain, and punctuation constraints as required for this specialized statistics task.\n```\n\nThe following are examples of different task inputs provided to the assistant along with the assistant's response for each of them, and some feedback on how the assistant's response could be better:\n```\n# Example 1\n## Inputs\nHi, can you tell me if these are cat or dog breeds thanks: Ragdoll, Beagle, Sphinx, Papillon, Pug, Abyssinian At the end of your response, please explicitly add a postscript starting with P.S.\n\n## Generated Outputs\nParagraph1-Ragdoll-is-a-cat-breed-it-is-known-for-its-large-size-and-blue-eyes-panic-Beagle-is-a-dog-breed-it-is-a-small-to-medium-sized-hound-known-for-its-scent-trailing-ability-panic-Sphinx-is-a-cat-breed-famous-for-being-hairless-and-having-wrinkled-skin-panic-Papillon-is-a-dog-breed-recognized-by-its-distinctive-butterfly-shaped-ears-panic-Pug-is-a-dog-breed-known-for-its-wrinkly-face-and-compact-body-panic-Abyssinian-is-a-cat-breed-noted-for-its-ticked-coat-pattern-panic-P.S-I-have-listed-each-breed-as-either-cat-or-dog-based-on-common-recognition-panic\n\n## Feedback\nSatisfied 0/1 constraints.\n✗ [detectable_content:postscript] FAILED — At the end of your response, please explicitly add a postscript starting with P.S.\n\n\n```\n\nYour task is to write a new instruction for the assistant.\n\nRead the inputs carefully and identify the input format and infer detailed task description about the task I wish to solve with the assistant.\n\nRead all the assistant responses and the corresponding feedback. Identify all niche and domain specific factual information about the task and include it in the instruction, as a lot of it may not be available to the assistant in the future. The assistant may have utilized a generalizable strategy to solve the task, if so, include that in the instruction as well.\n\nProvide the new instructions within ``` blocks."}, "raw_lm_outputs": {"system_prompt": "```\nYou are solving a probability and statistics word-problem involving the Poisson distribution and conditional probabi