t mixes trigonometric and exponential terms. Numerical methods (e.g., Newton-Raphson) can be used once the constants are specified.\n\n---\n\n### *5. Inequality for protesters exceeding officers by factor of at least 2*\n\nAlex desires to analyze when:\n\n\\[\nP(t) \\geq 2 O(t).\n\\]\n\nGiven the functions,\n\n\\[\nP_0 e^{kt} \\geq 2 \\left[ A \\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) + B \\cos(\\omega t + \\theta) \\right].\n\\]\n\nTo write this precisely:\n\n\\[\nP_0 e^{kt} - 2A \\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) - 2B \\cos(\\omega t + \\theta) \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### *6. General solution to the inequality*\n\nDefine:\n\n\\[\nF(t) = P_0 e^{kt} - 2A \\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) - 2B \\cos(\\omega t + \\theta).\n\\]\n\nWe want to find intervals where:\n\n\\[\nF(t) \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### *7. Analyzing the inequality*\n\n- The exponential \\(P_0 e^{kt}\\) is monotonic.\n- The trigonometric part oscillates between \\(-2\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}\\) and \\(2\\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}\\).\n- For sufficiently large \\(t\\), \\(P_0 e^{kt}\\) dominates, so \\(F(t) \\geq 0\\).\n\nSetting bounds:\n\n\\[\nP_0 e^{kt} \\geq 2 \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}.\n\\]\n\nTaking the natural logarithm (assuming \\(P_0 > 0\\), \\(k > 0\\)):\n\n\\[\nkt \\geq \\ln \\left(\\frac{2 \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}{P_0}\\right),\n\\]\n\\[\nt \\geq \\frac{1}{k} \\ln \\left( \\frac{2 \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}{P_0} \\right).\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### *Summary*\n\n- The explicit solution times for the derivative equality require numerical solving of:\n\\[\nA \\omega \\cos(\\omega t + \\phi) - B \\omega \\sin(\\omega t + \\theta) = k P_0 e^{kt}.\n\\]\n\n- The time intervals with protesters exceeding officers by a factor of at least 2 satisfy:\n\\[\nP_0 e^{kt} \\geq 2 A \\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) + 2 B \\cos(\\omega t + \\theta),\n\\]\nor equivalently,\n\\[\nP_0 e^{kt} - 2 A \\sin(\\omega t + \\phi) - 2 B \\cos(\\omega t + \\theta) \\geq 0.\n\\]\n\n- For large \\(t\\),\n\\[\nt \\geq \\frac{1}{k} \\ln \\left( \\frac{2 \\sqrt{A^2 + B^2}}{P_0} \\right),\n\\]\nensures that protesters