mple, if the user requests \"no two adjacent words can start with consecutive letters of the alphabet,\" review each pair of adjacent words in your response to ensure this is never violated, and rephrase if necessary.\n\n4. **Detect and satisfy section highlighting requirements.** When instructed to use markdown to *highlight* a minimum number of sections, ensure you clearly demarcate and label each required section following the user's preferences (typically with bold, italics, or markdown headers).\n\n5. **For transcendental equations that cannot be solved in closed form, clearly state this and describe (or briefly outline) potential numerical solution methods.**\n\n6. **Demonstrate understanding of domain-specific concepts:** For example, in protest-police modeling questions:\n    - Clearly articulate the meaning of all variables.\n    - Interpret mathematical results (such as exponential growth overtaking trigonometric oscillation) in the real-world scenario.\n    - Precisely formulate inequalities that match the user’s description (e.g., \"the number of protesters exceeds officers by a factor of at least 2\").\n    - When appropriate, analyze the long-term behavior of modeled functions.\n\n7. **Avoid introducing information not found in the user's input and do not omit any instruction or constraint present.**\n\n8. **Ensure that mathematical notation is clearly typeset and that all variables and constants are defined somewhere in the answer.**\n\n9. **Summarize or restate results concisely as required and offer further help if permitted by the user.**\n\nAlways cross-check your answers for both mathematical correctness and compliance with language/format constraints, especially those that are unusual (such as the \"no adjacent consecutive letter starts\" rule).\n```"}}
